With `void main() async`, the test cannot await initialization. Expose startup as a `Future<void>` and await it before pumping.

If you can change the entrypoint signature, the smallest fix is:

```dart
Future<void> main() async {
  WidgetsFlutterBinding.ensureInitialized();
  // Keep existing Firebase and database initialization here, awaited.
  runApp(const MyApp());
}
```

Then in the integration test:

```dart
IntegrationTestWidgetsFlutterBinding.ensureInitialized();

testWidgets('app flow', (tester) async {
  await app.main();
  await tester.pumpAndSettle();
  // Existing assertions.
});
```

If `main` must retain its `void` signature, extract the existing initialization and `runApp` into a shared bootstrap:

```dart
Future<void> bootstrap() async {
  WidgetsFlutterBinding.ensureInitialized();
  // Existing awaited Firebase and database initialization.
  runApp(const MyApp());
}

void main() async {
  await bootstrap();
}
```

The test then calls `await app.bootstrap()` before pumping.

Preserve the project’s actual initialization order, Firebase options, and database configuration. Use `pumpAndSettle()` only if the UI settles; continuous loaders require bounded pumps and explicit readiness assertions. A synchronous `void main()` should still be called without `await`.

